status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"demo-app.service (python3 http.server) was created, enabled, and started on node2, active on port 8080 (cmd-9b8774a3ffee45d8). Post-restart evidence confirms the service remains active and enabled (cmd-4491301c9a0749b7).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9b8774a3ffee45d8\",\"cmd-4491301c9a0749b7\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"WireGuard tunnel terminated on node1, with node3 as client\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"VyOS wg0 interface with keypair, listening on UDP 51820, and a peer entry for node3 was committed and saved on node1 (cmd-16407b46e97745d3, cmd-f9d6ec83398b4721). node3 installed wireguard, generated a keypair, and brought up wg-quick wg0 with a successful handshake to node1 (cmd-1e7d3bd497874559, cmd-5f47995fad87483b). Handshake and interface state were re-confirmed after node1 and node3 restarts (cmd-e5729907fe454d75, cmd-a0189c88393742c0).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-16407b46e97745d3\",\"cmd-f9d6ec83398b4721\",\"cmd-1e7d3bd497874559\",\"cmd-5f47995fad87483b\",\"cmd-e5729907fe454d75\",\"cmd-a0189c88393742c0\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node3 (outside the private network) can reach node2:8080 only through the WireGuard tunnel via node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An early direct-access probe (cmd-a035a583c81a4913) was inconclusive because node3 had a more-specific route to node2 via wg0 even during a 'direct' test, so it doesn't isolate the two paths. However, a proper bounded test later disables wg0 entirely and shows the direct path fails (http_code=000, cmd-e277ee9fb2a843d0), then re-enables wg0 and shows the same request succeeds (cmd-7d25275d968c46d5). node2's iptables ACCEPT/DROP ordering was corrected (cmd-f79c82a165bb475b) so only node1's masqueraded source IP is permitted (cmd-b0936ce8c236446b), consistent with NAT masquerade on node1 (source 10.200.0.0/24 -> eth0), which is why tunnel traffic is accepted while raw node3 traffic is dropped. This is confirmed again after node2 and node3 restarts (cmd-1aca9a0a192a458c, cmd-4d1a859ec1b04348).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e277ee9fb2a843d0\",\"cmd-7d25275d968c46d5\",\"cmd-b0936ce8c236446b\",\"cmd-f79c82a165bb475b\",\"cmd-1aca9a0a192a458c\",\"cmd-4d1a859ec1b04348\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all managed nodes (node1, node2, node3), each showing pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show pre-restart boot_id capture, a restart action, polling until reachable, a distinct post-restart boot_id, and functional recovery: node1 (7668189d->ed9ce6dd, wg0/NAT restored and data path re-verified via cmd-5bd90a5b6ee94a47), node2 (dca72801->08f9226c, demo-app and iptables-restore services active, app reachable from node1's IP), node3 (74fd91b6->4fd06daf, wg-quick@wg0 auto-started with fresh handshake and app reachable). This matches the fixed lifecycle before_prepare/after_executor boot-identity snapshots for all three nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-05726091b10d4b36\",\"cmd-e897dc0ff76a48f6\",\"cmd-d88d8636be9a44d8\",\"cmd-e5729907fe454d75\",\"cmd-5bd90a5b6ee94a47\",\"cmd-d8da419483084839\",\"cmd-72bdc2972e66456a\",\"cmd-ef90dd51aa5b42e4\",\"cmd-4491301c9a0749b7\",\"cmd-4d1a859ec1b04348\",\"cmd-d23606e6f36e4395\",\"cmd-28e04c0515164827\",\"cmd-96d1da8bbbe8436d\",\"cmd-a0189c88393742c0\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map directly to the task or the mandatory restart protocol. Two minor issues: (1) cmd-fb8c9c5c380f4eab generated a VyOS PKI-installed WireGuard keypair on node1 that was never used in the final config (superseded by a manually specified key from cmd-b88967ea68a74554); this leaves an unused key artifact in the PKI store but has no functional or security impact. (2) cmd-cbfa7c0827044c33 (apt-get install iptables-persistent) hung and timed out; the executor killed the stuck apt processes (cmd-c7277e32b8e74788) and ran dpkg --configure -a to repair package state, then proceeded without iptables-persistent, using a custom iptables-restore.service instead. This was a bounded, fully-corrected troubleshooting excursion with no residual package damage (confirmed clean dpkg state in after_executor package-health snapshots). No other unnecessary, broad, or destructive mutations were observed; iptables rule ordering was promptly corrected once found to be reversed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fb8c9c5c380f4eab\",\"cmd-b88967ea68a74554\",\"cmd-cbfa7c0827044c33\",\"cmd-c7277e32b8e74788\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor deployed a Python http.server on node2:8080 via systemd, terminated a WireGuard tunnel on node1 (VyOS) with node3 as the sole client, and enforced tunnel-only access via node2 iptables rules combined with node1's NAT masquerade of tunnel traffic. All three material outcomes are supported by direct, coherent evidence, including a valid bounded down/up test isolating the tunnel-only access path (an earlier probe was flawed due to routing but was superseded by a correct test). The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed on all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity divergence and full functional recovery, consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"The first direct-access test (cmd-a035a583c81a4913) is not reliable evidence of isolation because node3 had a route to node2 via wg0 even during that 'direct' attempt; the isolation claim rests on the later, methodologically sound down/up test.\",\"No packet-capture or explicit source-IP verification was shown proving that NAT masquerade (not some other path) is what allows tunnel traffic through node2's firewall; this is inferred from configuration and behavioral testing rather than directly observed on the wire.\",\"Node2's pre-restart healthy baseline for iptables/app state was not separately captured as a distinct pre-restart command in the timeline beyond the general boot-identity/uptime check, though earlier verification commands (cmd-b0936ce8c236446b, cmd-f79c82a165bb475b) establish it shortly before the restart sequence.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Serve an application from node2 on TCP port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"demo-app.service (python3 http.server) was created, enabled, and started on node2, active on port 8080 (cmd-9b8774a3ffee45d8). Post-restart evidence confirms the service remains active and enabled (cmd-4491301c9a0749b7).","evidence_ids":["cmd-9b8774a3ffee45d8","cmd-4491301c9a0749b7"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"WireGuard tunnel terminated on node1, with node3 as client","status":"satisfied","summary":"VyOS wg0 interface with keypair, listening on UDP 51820, and a peer entry for node3 was committed and saved on node1 (cmd-16407b46e97745d3, cmd-f9d6ec83398b4721). node3 installed wireguard, generated a keypair, and brought up wg-quick wg0 with a successful handshake to node1 (cmd-1e7d3bd497874559, cmd-5f47995fad87483b). Handshake and interface state were re-confirmed after node1 and node3 restarts (cmd-e5729907fe454d75, cmd-a0189c88393742c0).","evidence_ids":["cmd-16407b46e97745d3","cmd-f9d6ec83398b4721","cmd-1e7d3bd497874559","cmd-5f47995fad87483b","cmd-e5729907fe454d75","cmd-a0189c88393742c0"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"node3 (outside the private network) can reach node2:8080 only through the WireGuard tunnel via node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"An early direct-access probe (cmd-a035a583c81a4913) was incon